  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 156 PM EDT Wed Sep 9 2026\n\nEarly afternoon water vapor imagery and RAP analysis shows a dry \nairmass spreading across the UP amid broad troughing, while a \nsharper trough is rotating west to east across Ontario. At the \nsurface, an unseasonably strong, 980 mb low (1 percentile of climo) \nis located over southern Hudson Bay. Strong low-level height \ngradient associated with this circulation resulting in 30-40 kt \nwesterlies at 850 mb combined with deep mixing is promoting a breezy \nday, especially across the Keweenaw which is closer to the 850 mb \nwind core. 35-40 kt wind gusts will be common there the remainder of \ntoday, with 25-30 kt elsewhere. \n\nRidging and a dry airmass (PWATs falling to below 0.75 in) promote a \ncooler night tonight with lows near normal in the mid-40s to mid-\n50s. Remaining seasonable and clear on Thursday with highs in the \nlow to mid-70s. \n\nSeasonably strong 500 mb zonal flow evolves Fri/Sat over the \nnorthern tier of the western half of the CONUS. A lead shortwave \ntrough ejecting across the Upper Midwest will foster a compact \nsurface low/front over the northern Plains, near which convection is \nexpected to erupt Friday afternoon/evening within a plume of \nmoderate instability. Southerly flow ahead of this surface feature \nwill promote a warm and dry day Friday with highs around 80. The \nfront sweeps across the UP Friday night with rain and thunderstorms \nexpected for the west half, weakening with eastward extent along \nwith the instability. Severe weather is not expected at this time \ndue to the waning instability. It will be more seasonable and \ndry behind the front on Saturday and likely into Sunday. \n\nLooking ahead into early next week, there are indications that a \nwetter pattern evolves as a Colorado low develops in response to \ndeep troughing over the northern Rockies. Good poleward Gulf \nmoisture advection is modeled (mean PWATs in the 1.5-1.75 inch range \nlocally per the LREF, which is toward the 95 pctl of climo), which \nwill be lifted atop a low-level frontal boundary to provide a \npotentially long-lived period of rainfall Monday into Tuesday in the \nUpper Midwest. Cluster analysis shows the main mode of variability \nis tied to the overall amplification of the encroaching trough and \ndownstream ridge, with a more (less) amplified height field favoring \na wetter (drier) solution. About a quarter of the LREF members are \nslower with the trough, which would favor another dry day on Monday, \nas well. Regardless, LREF indicates a 50-60% chance of accumulating \n1\" of rainfall by Tuesday evening, and a 20-30% chance of 2\". Medium \nrange AI-based convective hazards forecasts indicate any severe \nweather risk remains to our south during this period.",
